- Florian Lonsing, Vienna University of Technology (DepQBF)
- Max Heisinger (Web port, CMake, Emscripten compilation: https://github.com/maximaximal/limboole)

Fork Updates by mrbana69 (https://github.com/mrbana69/limboole):
- Redesigned developer-tool UI (editor-first, compact workspace, light/dark themes)
- Multi-language localization (14 web languages: EN, ES, DE, IT, FR, PT, ZH, JA, KO, RU, AR, NL, PL, TR)
- Truth table generator (-t)
- Extended operators: XOR (^), boolean constants (true, false, 1, 0), alternative OR (/)
- Python bindings & package (import limboole)
- Structured JSON output (-j)
- Automated CI workflow for Linux, Windows, macOS

Installing & Python Usage
-------------------------

    pip install .

    import limboole

    # Check validity (tautology)
    limboole.is_valid("p | !p")       # True
    limboole.is_valid("p & q")        # False

    # Check satisfiability
    limboole.is_sat("p & q")          # True
    limboole.is_sat("p & !p")         # False

    # Truth table generation
    print(limboole.truth_table("p ^ q"))

CLI Usage & Options
-------------------

    limboole [ <option> ... ] [ <in-file> ]

    -h, --help     print command line summary and exit
    --version      print the version and exit
    -v             increase verbosity
    -p             pretty print input formula only
    -d             dump generated CNF only
    -s             check satisfiability (default: validity)
    -t, --truth-table print ASCII truth table of the formula
    -j, --json     output result in JSON format
    -o <out-file>  set output file (default: stdout)
    -l <log-file>  set log file (default: stderr)
    --picosat      use PicoSAT SAT solver back-end
    --depqbf       use DepQBF QBF solver back-end

Language Syntax
---------------

The input format has the following syntax in BNF:
( [ ... ] means optional,  { ... } means repeated arbitrary many times)
   
   expr    ::= iff
   iff     ::= implies { '<->' implies }
   implies ::= or [ '->' or | '<-' or ]
   or      ::= xor { ('|' | '/') xor }
   xor     ::= and { '^' and }
   and     ::= not { '&' not }
   not     ::= basic | '!' not | '~' not | '-' not
   basic   ::= var | const | '(' expr ')'
   const   ::= 'true' | 'false' | '1' | '0'

and 'var' is a string over letters, digits and the following characters:
  
  - _ . [ ] $ @

The last character of 'var' should be different from '-'.
